// Stdlib: FEA Material Traits
//
// Provides the dimensioned `ElasticMaterial` trait + a starter library of four
// canonical engineering materials (Steel_AISI_1045, Aluminium_6061_T6,
// Titanium_Ti6Al4V, ABS_Plastic) needed by the v0.3 structural-analysis FEA
// solver. Each material carries per-property provenance metadata referencing
// a public reference (matweb / MMPDS / ASM Handbook).
//
// PRD reference: docs/prds/v0_3/structural-analysis-fea.md task #1.
//
// Module exports:
//   - `MaterialPropertyProvenance` — citation record (source, reference, notes)
//   - `ElasticMaterial`            — dimensioned FEA trait
//   - `Damped`                     — free-standing hysteretic-loss mixin (η)
//   - `DampedMaterial`             — named intersection `ElasticMaterial + Damped`
//   - `Steel_AISI_1045`            — medium-carbon steel (general-purpose)
//   - `Aluminium_6061_T6`          — precipitation-hardened aerospace aluminium
//   - `Titanium_Ti6Al4V`           — alpha-beta titanium (aerospace, biomedical)
//   - `ABS_Plastic`                — general-purpose moulded thermoplastic
//
// Citation policy: every property value cites a public, durable reference —
// typically matweb's online material property database, the MMPDS metallic
// design data handbook, or the ASM Handbook series. Each property also
// carries a free-text `notes` field describing any caveats (heat treatment,
// alloy variant, ductile-to-brittle transition, etc.) so downstream FEA
// reports can reproduce the citation chain. Per-material citation sources
// are tagged in a comment line directly above each `structure def`.
//
// Relationship to existing material traits:
//   - This module's `ElasticMaterial` is a NEW, dimensioned trait designed
//     for the FEA solver. It extends `ConstitutiveLaw`, adds `density`
//     and `yield_stress` alongside `youngs_modulus`/`poisson_ratio`. See
//     the trait doc below for `poisson_ratio`'s range and rationale.
//   - The pre-existing `Elastic` trait in `materials_mechanical.ri` is
//     already dimensioned too: `youngs_modulus`/`shear_modulus` are
//     `Pressure`, and only `poissons_ratio` is `Real` (correctly so — it's
//     a dimensionless ratio). It mixes an optional `shear_modulus`
//     alongside `youngs_modulus`, and constrains `poissons_ratio` to the
//     open interval `(0, 0.5)` — a narrower range than `ElasticMaterial`'s,
//     not an absent one. It is retained verbatim for legacy consumers —
//     existing conformers in `examples/` and the compiler test fixtures.
//   - The two traits also spell the ratio param differently:
//     `poissons_ratio` on `Elastic` vs `poisson_ratio` on
//     `ElasticMaterial`. A structure conforming to both traits must
//     declare both params — porting between them on name alone raises
//     `MissingRequiredMember`.
//   - Neither trait supersedes the other; they coexist for different
//     purposes. The real justification for the split is `ConstitutiveLaw`
//     conformance and a single solver-facing trait: `solve_elastic_static`
//     accepts a `material : ConstitutiveLaw` param, so only a
//     `ConstitutiveLaw` conformer type-checks there, and `ElasticMaterial`
//     bundles `density`/`yield_stress` into that one trait, rather than
//     composing the narrow single-concern traits `materials_mechanical.ri`
//     is built around (`MaterialSpec` for `density`, `Elastic` for E/ν,
//     `Strong` for `yield_strength`). It is NOT dimension-typing (both
//     traits are now dimensioned) or the Poisson range (a minor,
//     `Elastic`-is-narrower difference). New FEA-bound code should
//     depend on `ElasticMaterial`.

// ── Provenance ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// MaterialPropertyProvenance is the citation record attached to a single
// property value of a concrete material structure. Each material in this
// module carries four `..._provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance` fields
// (one per property) so that downstream FEA reports can reproduce the
// citation chain end-to-end.
//
//   source    — short tag identifying the data origin (e.g. "matweb",
//               "MMPDS", "ASM Handbook").
//   reference — the specific record/entry within `source` (alloy + temper,
//               handbook chapter, table number, etc.).
//   notes     — free-text caveats: heat treatment range, alloy variant,
//               ductile-to-brittle transition, averaging assumptions.
//
// Declared above all trait blocks so the structure-name set built in the
// pre-pass sees `MaterialPropertyProvenance` before any material structure
// references it as a `param ..._provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance`
// type. This mirrors the `Provenance` placement convention in `io.ri`.
structure def MaterialPropertyProvenance {
    param source : String
    param reference : String
    param notes : String
}

// ── ConstitutiveLaw marker ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ConstitutiveLaw is the marker trait conformed to by every concrete
// constitutive law (isotropic, orthotropic, transverse-isotropic). The
// body is intentionally empty: the contract "produce a 6×6 stiffness D
// in the material's local frame" is implemented producer-side in Rust
// (`reify-solver-elastic::constitutive`) and dispatched on the
// structure-name carried by `Value::StructureInstance`. The stdlib trait
// exists so `param material : ConstitutiveLaw` resolves in user code
// (e.g. `AnisotropicMaterial.law` below and `solve_elastic_static`) and
// so downstream PRDs can declare their own conforming structures.
//
// Empty-marker pattern follows `trait Load { }` in std.fea.types
// (relaxed in DD1; see fea_types.ri).
//
// Declared here (above ElasticMaterial) rather than in constitutive.ri so
// that `trait ElasticMaterial : ConstitutiveLaw` is not a forward-reference
// (materials_fea loads before constitutive in stdlib_loader.rs — PRD §4.2 γ).
// constitutive.ri retains a pointer comment; Orthotropic/TransverseIsotropic
// still refine ConstitutiveLaw via the growing prelude.
trait ConstitutiveLaw { }

// ── ElasticMaterial trait ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ElasticMaterial is the dimensioned trait the v0.3 FEA solver consumes. It
// declares the minimum set of constitutive properties needed for linear
// isotropic elasticity (small-strain regime) plus an optional yield-stress
// hook for downstream plasticity/limit-state checks.
//
// Required properties:
//   youngs_modulus — Young's modulus E.  SI unit: pascal (kg·m⁻¹·s⁻²).
//                    Slope of the uniaxial stress/strain curve in the
//                    linear-elastic regime.
//   poisson_ratio  — Poisson's ratio ν.  Dimensionless ratio of transverse
//                    to axial strain under uniaxial load.  Constrained to
//                    the half-open interval [0, 0.5); ν = 0.5 represents
//                    incompressibility and breaks the linear-elastic bulk
//                    modulus formula K = E / (3(1 − 2ν)) (singularity).
//   density        — mass density ρ.  SI unit: kg·m⁻³.  Used by inertial
//                    and modal analyses; required even for static FEA so
//                    the same material spec works across solvers.
//   yield_stress   — uniaxial tensile yield stress σy.  SI unit: pascal.
//                    Optional because purely linear-elastic FEA does not
//                    consult it; downstream plasticity / limit-state /
//                    safety-factor passes do.
trait ElasticMaterial : ConstitutiveLaw {
    param youngs_modulus : Pressure
    param poisson_ratio : Real // dimensionless
    param density : Density
    param yield_stress : Option<Pressure>

    // Lower bound: ν >= 0 — auxetic materials (ν < 0) are excluded from the
    // FEA-bound trait. They exist physically but require specialised
    // constitutive handling outside the linear-isotropic regime.
    constraint poisson_ratio >= 0
    // Upper bound: ν < 0.5 (HALF-OPEN). Exactly 0.5 represents
    // incompressibility and breaks the linear-elastic bulk-modulus formula
    //   K = E / (3(1 − 2ν))
    // which goes singular as ν → 0.5. Use a separate hyper-elastic /
    // mixed-formulation solver for incompressible materials (e.g. rubber).
    constraint poisson_ratio < 0.5
}


// ── Damped mixin + DampedMaterial named intersection ─────────────────────────
//
// PRD reference: docs/prds/v0_6/damped-modal-bonded-heterogeneous.md
// §Contract C1, §Resolved decisions 1-2 (leaf α, task #6877).

// `Damped` carries the single hysteretic-loss input the damped-modal solve
// reads.
//
//   loss_factor — structural (hysteretic) loss factor η. Dimensionless ratio
//                 of energy dissipated per radian to peak strain energy
//                 stored, i.e. the imaginary part of the complex modulus
//                 E* = E(1 + iη). At (or near) a resonance the equivalent
//                 viscous damping ratio is ζ ≈ η/2, which is the conversion
//                 the modal solve applies per mode.
//
// η ≥ 0 is normative and is pinned by the constraint below rather than by
// this comment — a negative loss factor is energy generation, not damping.
// The bound mirrors `ElasticMaterial`'s two Poisson constraints above; both
// inject into every conformer through the same trait-constraint pass.
//
// `Damped` is a FREE-STANDING mixin: it deliberately does NOT refine
// `ConstitutiveLaw`. Loss is orthogonal to the stiffness law, so keeping the
// mixin parentless lets it compose with future non-isotropic damped laws
// (Orthotropic / TransverseIsotropic) instead of being welded to the
// linear-isotropic family (PRD decision 1).
//
// ── NAMING HAZARD — `Damped` (here) vs `Damping` (materials_mechanical.ri) ──
//
// These two are unrelated, and neither supersedes nor refines the other.
// Picking one by name alone is the mistake this note exists to prevent:
//
//   - `Damped` (HERE, std/materials/fea) is the CONSTITUTIVE / FEA loss
//     input. A free-standing mixin with a single `loss_factor`, consumed by
//     the damped-modal solve, composed into `DampedMaterial` below alongside
//     the `ConstitutiveLaw` family.
//   - `trait Damping : MaterialSpec { damping_ratio; loss_factor }` in
//     `crates/reify-compiler/stdlib/materials_mechanical.ri:183` is a
//     DATASHEET-SPEC trait in the `MaterialSpec` family — the shape a
//     supplier datasheet populates, carrying `damping_ratio` as well. Real
//     conformer: `examples/drivebelt_trait_bounds.ri:48`.
//
// The interaction is benign: a structure conforming to BOTH gets exactly one
// `loss_factor` requirement, because `collect_all_requirements` dedups by
// (name, kind) and both traits spell it `Real`.
// (Reciprocal note lives above `trait Damping` in materials_mechanical.ri.)
trait Damped {
    param loss_factor : Real // dimensionless

    // Lower bound: η >= 0. A negative loss factor would mean the material
    // adds energy over a cycle rather than dissipating it. Undamped (η = 0)
    // is admissible and is the degenerate case the undamped modal solve
    // already handles.
    constraint loss_factor >= 0
}

// `DampedMaterial` is the ergonomic named intersection for the isotropic
// damped case — the spelling a user writes as
// `structure def MySteel : DampedMaterial + Visual { … }` (PRD §Goal).
//
// The body is EMPTY on purpose. Requirements and trait constraints are
// collected transitively through the refinement chain
// (`collect_all_requirements`, trait_requirements.rs:152), so a conformer
// must declare BOTH parents' params — `youngs_modulus`, `poisson_ratio`,
// `density`, `yield_stress` from `ElasticMaterial` plus `loss_factor` from
// `Damped` — and inherits all three trait constraints (the two Poisson
// bounds and η >= 0). Restating any member here would duplicate a contract
// the compiler already derives.
//
// Multi-refinement idiom precedent: `trait Watertight : Closed + Manifold {}`
// in `stdlib/geometry_traits.ri:84`.
//
// Conformance is transitive in the other direction too: a `DampedMaterial`
// conformer still satisfies `ElasticMaterial`, `Damped` and (via
// `ElasticMaterial`) `ConstitutiveLaw`, so every existing consumer of those
// bounds keeps type-checking unchanged.
trait DampedMaterial : ElasticMaterial + Damped {}
// ── Starter material library ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each concrete material below is a top-level structure conforming to
// `DampedMaterial` — the named intersection `ElasticMaterial + Damped`
// declared above (α #6877, PRD §Goal / decision 2). The presets conform to
// the intersection rather than spelling both parents out.
//
// Nothing downstream changes at compile time: `DampedMaterial` REFINES
// `ElasticMaterial`, so the four elastic properties and both Poisson
// constraints still arrive transitively (via `collect_all_requirements`),
// and every `ElasticMaterial` / `ConstitutiveLaw` consumer — `solve_elastic_static`,
// `solve_buckling`, `stdlib/fdm.ri:153` — still accepts a preset, because
// `trait_satisfies` walks all parents of a multi-parent refinement.
//
// Note that `TopologyTemplate.trait_bounds` is DECLARED-only, so a preset's
// bounds vec now reads `["DampedMaterial", "Visual"]`: "ElasticMaterial" is
// legitimately absent there while remaining satisfied. Tests that need the
// satisfies property must assert it at a trait-bounded slot, not by
// inspecting that vec.
//
// Defaults bake in matweb-equivalent public-reference
// values so a bare `Steel_AISI_1045()` instantiation populates every cell;
// callers may override individual fields at construction (e.g.
// `Steel_AISI_1045(yield_stress: some(550MPa))` for a tempered variant).
//
// Per-property provenance is encoded as five parallel `..._provenance`
// fields — one per property, including α's (#6877) `loss_factor` — rather
// than a single `Map<String, ...>` so:
//   - typos at the source level become missing-member errors
//   - downstream FEA reports can pull each citation by name
//   - refining traits that add properties (shear modulus, hardening, ...)
//     compose cleanly with the same parallel-slot convention
//
// Density literals use the compound-unit form `<n>kg/m^3` (resolved by
// `resolve_unit_expr`; PRD docs/prds/unit-expressions.md §4). This module
// compiles after std.units, so the unit registry is in scope and the compound
// literal folds to a single dimensioned value (kg·m⁻³).

// MMPDS / matweb citation: AISI 1045, hot-rolled, medium-carbon steel.
//
// Steel_AISI_1045 is a general-purpose medium-carbon steel widely used for
// shafts, axles, and machined parts. Properties below are typical-room-
// temperature values for the hot-rolled, normalised condition; tempered
// or quenched-and-tempered variants will exhibit higher yield_stress.
//
// γ (#4762): conforms to Visual; appearance is EDITORIAL (mid-dark neutral
// grey, slight satin sheen) — NOT physically derived (PRD §4.3 decision 5).
structure def Steel_AISI_1045 : DampedMaterial + Visual {
    param youngs_modulus : Pressure = 205GPa
    param poisson_ratio : Real = 0.29 // dimensionless
    param density : Density = 7850kg/m^3
    param yield_stress : Option<Pressure> = some(310MPa)
    param loss_factor : Real = 0.0006 // dimensionless (eta) — class-level, see provenance

    param youngs_modulus_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "AISI 1045 Steel, hot rolled, 19-32 mm round",
            notes: "Average across heat treatments — matweb material property database. Typical room-temperature value for medium-carbon steel."
        )
    param poisson_ratio_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "AISI 1045 Steel, hot rolled, 19-32 mm round",
            notes: "Typical for plain-carbon steel; insensitive to heat treatment."
        )
    param density_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "AISI 1045 Steel, hot rolled, 19-32 mm round",
            notes: "Nominal density of plain-carbon steel."
        )
    param yield_stress_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "AISI 1045 Steel, hot rolled, 19-32 mm round",
            notes: "Tensile yield strength for hot-rolled condition; quenched-and-tempered variants reach higher values."
        )
    param loss_factor_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "material-class range (engineering handbook / vibration-damping literature)",
            reference: "Structural / plain-carbon steel — material-class hysteretic loss factor, not an AISI 1045 datasheet value",
            notes: "CLASS-LEVEL structural / plain-carbon steel loss factor, NOT an AISI-1045-specific measurement: published values for structural steel span roughly 2e-4 to 1e-3 and 6e-4 is an order-of-magnitude representative. Eta is amplitude-dependent (pronounced in metals at low stress) and, at or near a resonance, converts as zeta ~= eta/2. Design-critical damping predictions need a measurement of the specific alloy, temper and joint condition, not this class value."
        )

    // Editorial appearance: mid-dark neutral grey, slight satin sheen.
    // Values are EDITORIAL (PRD §4.3 decision 5 — NOT physically derived).
    param appearance : Appearance = Appearance(
        color: Color(r: 0.50, g: 0.50, b: 0.52),
        finish: Finish.Satin,
        metalness: 0.90,
        roughness: 0.40
    )
}

// matweb citation: Aluminum 6061-T6, precipitation-hardened aerospace alloy.
//
// Aluminium_6061_T6 is the workhorse aerospace and structural aluminium
// alloy. The T6 temper denotes solution-heat-treatment followed by
// artificial ageing. Properties below are typical room-temperature values;
// exposure to elevated temperatures degrades the precipitation hardening.
//
// γ (#4762): conforms to Visual; appearance is EDITORIAL (lighter grey, satin)
// — NOT physically derived (PRD §4.3 decision 5).
structure def Aluminium_6061_T6 : DampedMaterial + Visual {
    param youngs_modulus : Pressure = 68.9GPa
    param poisson_ratio : Real = 0.33 // dimensionless
    param density : Density = 2700kg/m^3
    param yield_stress : Option<Pressure> = some(276MPa)
    param loss_factor : Real = 0.0002 // dimensionless (eta) — class-level, see provenance

    param youngs_modulus_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Aluminum 6061-T6",
            notes: "Typical room-temperature Young's modulus for 6061-T6 wrought aluminium."
        )
    param poisson_ratio_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Aluminum 6061-T6",
            notes: "Typical for wrought aluminium alloys; insensitive to temper."
        )
    param density_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Aluminum 6061-T6",
            notes: "Nominal density of 6061 wrought aluminium alloy."
        )
    param yield_stress_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Aluminum 6061-T6",
            notes: "Tensile yield strength for T6 temper; degrades on prolonged exposure above ~150 °C."
        )
    param loss_factor_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "material-class range (engineering handbook / vibration-damping literature)",
            reference: "Wrought aluminium alloys — material-class hysteretic loss factor, not a 6061-T6 datasheet value",
            notes: "CLASS-LEVEL wrought-aluminium loss factor, NOT a 6061-T6-specific measurement: published values for wrought aluminium alloys span roughly 1e-4 to 4e-4 and 2e-4 is an order-of-magnitude representative. Eta is amplitude-dependent (pronounced in metals at low stress) and, at or near a resonance, converts as zeta ~= eta/2. Design-critical damping predictions need a measurement of the specific alloy, temper and joint condition, not this class value."
        )

    // Editorial appearance: lighter grey, satin.
    // Values are EDITORIAL (PRD §4.3 decision 5 — NOT physically derived).
    param appearance : Appearance = Appearance(
        color: Color(r: 0.66, g: 0.67, b: 0.69),
        finish: Finish.Satin,
        metalness: 0.90,
        roughness: 0.45
    )
}

// matweb / ASM Handbook citation: Titanium Ti-6Al-4V (Grade 5), annealed.
//
// Titanium_Ti6Al4V is the alpha-beta titanium alloy that dominates aerospace
// and biomedical-implant applications. Properties below are for the annealed
// condition. Solution-treated-and-aged (STA) variants reach higher
// yield_stress at the cost of reduced ductility.
//
// γ (#4762): conforms to Visual; appearance is EDITORIAL (grey, satin)
// — NOT physically derived (PRD §4.3 decision 5).
structure def Titanium_Ti6Al4V : DampedMaterial + Visual {
    param youngs_modulus : Pressure = 113.8GPa
    param poisson_ratio : Real = 0.342 // dimensionless
    param density : Density = 4430kg/m^3
    param yield_stress : Option<Pressure> = some(880MPa)
    param loss_factor : Real = 0.0004 // dimensionless (eta) — class-level, see provenance

    param youngs_modulus_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Titanium Ti-6Al-4V (Grade 5), Annealed",
            notes: "Typical room-temperature Young's modulus for Grade 5 titanium."
        )
    param poisson_ratio_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Titanium Ti-6Al-4V (Grade 5), Annealed",
            notes: "Typical for alpha-beta titanium alloys."
        )
    param density_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Titanium Ti-6Al-4V (Grade 5), Annealed",
            notes: "Nominal density of Ti-6Al-4V."
        )
    param yield_stress_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Titanium Ti-6Al-4V (Grade 5), Annealed",
            notes: "Tensile yield for annealed condition; STA temper reaches higher values."
        )
    param loss_factor_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "material-class range (engineering handbook / vibration-damping literature)",
            reference: "Alpha-beta titanium alloys — material-class hysteretic loss factor, not a Ti-6Al-4V datasheet value",
            notes: "CLASS-LEVEL alpha-beta titanium loss factor, NOT a Ti-6Al-4V-specific measurement: published values for alpha-beta titanium alloys span roughly 1e-4 to 1e-3 and 4e-4 is an order-of-magnitude representative. Eta is amplitude-dependent (pronounced in metals at low stress) and, at or near a resonance, converts as zeta ~= eta/2. Design-critical damping predictions need a measurement of the specific alloy, temper and joint condition, not this class value."
        )

    // Editorial appearance: grey, satin.
    // Values are EDITORIAL (PRD §4.3 decision 5 — NOT physically derived).
    param appearance : Appearance = Appearance(
        color: Color(r: 0.55, g: 0.54, b: 0.53),
        finish: Finish.Satin,
        metalness: 0.85,
        roughness: 0.45
    )
}

// matweb citation: ABS, general-purpose, moulded grade.
//
// ABS_Plastic is the general-purpose acrylonitrile-butadiene-styrene
// thermoplastic used in injection moulding and FDM 3D printing. Properties
// below are room-temperature values for general-purpose moulded ABS.
// Yield_stress is approximate because ABS exhibits a ductile-to-brittle
// transition that depends on strain rate and temperature; for FEA-grade
// limit-state work, refer to the supplier's datasheet for the specific
// resin grade.
//
// γ (#4762): conforms to Visual; appearance is EDITORIAL (matte off-white)
// — NOT physically derived (PRD §4.3 decision 5).
structure def ABS_Plastic : DampedMaterial + Visual {
    param youngs_modulus : Pressure = 2.3GPa
    param poisson_ratio : Real = 0.35 // dimensionless
    param density : Density = 1050kg/m^3
    param yield_stress : Option<Pressure> = some(40MPa)
    param loss_factor : Real = 0.02 // dimensionless (eta) — class-level, see provenance

    param youngs_modulus_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Acrylonitrile Butadiene Styrene (ABS), General Purpose, Molded",
            notes: "Typical room-temperature tensile modulus for general-purpose moulded ABS."
        )
    param poisson_ratio_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Acrylonitrile Butadiene Styrene (ABS), General Purpose, Molded",
            notes: "Typical for amorphous thermoplastics in the linear-elastic regime."
        )
    param density_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Acrylonitrile Butadiene Styrene (ABS), General Purpose, Molded",
            notes: "Nominal density for general-purpose ABS resins (varies with filler content)."
        )
    param yield_stress_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "matweb",
            reference: "Acrylonitrile Butadiene Styrene (ABS), General Purpose, Molded",
            notes: "Approximate yield: ABS exhibits a ductile-to-brittle transition sensitive to strain rate and temperature. Refer to supplier datasheet for limit-state work."
        )
    param loss_factor_provenance : MaterialPropertyProvenance =
        MaterialPropertyProvenance(
            source: "material-class range (engineering handbook / vibration-damping literature)",
            reference: "Amorphous thermoplastics — material-class hysteretic loss factor, not an ABS resin-grade datasheet value",
            notes: "CLASS-LEVEL amorphous-thermoplastic loss factor, NOT a resin-grade-specific measurement: published values for amorphous thermoplastics span roughly 1e-2 to 4e-2 and are STRONGLY frequency- and temperature-dependent (eta rises steeply approaching the glass transition); 2e-2 is an order-of-magnitude representative. Eta is amplitude-dependent (pronounced in metals at low stress) and, at or near a resonance, converts as zeta ~= eta/2. Design-critical damping predictions need a measurement of the specific alloy, temper and joint condition, not this class value."
        )

    // Editorial appearance: matte off-white.
    // Values are EDITORIAL (PRD §4.3 decision 5 — NOT physically derived).
    param appearance : Appearance = Appearance(
        color: Color(r: 0.92, g: 0.91, b: 0.88),
        finish: Finish.Matte,
        metalness: 0.0,
        roughness: 0.85
    )
}
